/* phase F und_r13: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["13·nav"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["13·nav"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"13·nav.1.1","q":"What are L, R and T for a rise, and what does a tide table know?","a":"L is the height at low water, R the range of the rise and T how long the rise takes; the table is a function of time known only at the times it lists","w":["L is the length of the wharf, R the rate and T the tide; the table knows the height at every instant","L is the low, R the high and T the hour of the day; the table knows the rate at each hour","L is the latest reading, R the next and T their gap; the table knows why the water rises"],"why":"Monterey's evening: L = 2.77 ft, R = 3.70 ft, T = 6.18 h. Between its times the water rises without jumps, and the table says nothing about how."},
 {"u":"13·nav.2.1","q":"What does the centred-chord technique say, and what does it give for 7 p.m. at Monterey?","a":"for a constant plus a multiple of t plus a multiple of t², the chord over a stretch centred on t has exactly the slope at t, and for other functions it is a close guess; the chord from 6 to 8 gives about 0.91 ft an hour","w":["the chord from t forward has exactly the slope at t for every function; the chord from 7 to 8 gives 0.64 ft an hour","the chord over the whole rise has the slope at every t; it gives 0.60 ft an hour","a centred chord is always too steep; it gives 1.82 ft an hour"],"why":"(5.651 − 3.830)/2 ≈ 0.91. The chord from 6 to 7 belongs to 6:30 more than to either end."},
 {"u":"13·nav.3.1","q":"What is the smooth rise, and what is its rate?","a":"h(t) = L + R(3s² − 2s³), with s the share of the rise gone by: it starts at rest at L and ends at rest at L + R; its rate is (6R/T)(s − s²)","w":["h(t) = L + R · s, a linear function; its rate is R/T all through","h(t) = L + R · s²; its rate is 2Rs","h(t) = R(3s² − 2s³) − L; its rate is 6R(s − s²), with no T"],"why":"The plainest polynomial with no corners that starts and ends at rest has degree 3. The inside s = (t − t₀)/T brings the factor 1/T."},
 {"u":"13·nav.4.1","q":"What does fastest-at-mid-tide say, and why is it so?","a":"the smooth rise's rate is greatest exactly halfway, where it is 3R/(2T), one and a half times the average; s − s² is ¼ less a square, so it is greatest only at s = ½","w":["the rate is greatest at low water, where it is R/T; the water has furthest to go","the rate is the same all through, R/T; the rise is a linear function","the rate is greatest at high water, where it is 6R/T; s − s² grows with s"],"why":"Completing the square: s − s² = ¼ − (s − ½)². The rise must start and end at rest and still cover R in the time T, so the middle makes it up."},
 {"u":"13·nav.5.1","q":"What is the rule of twelfths, and how does it compare with the smooth rise?","a":"over a rise of about six hours the water climbs 1, 2, 3, 3, 2, 1 twelfths of the range hour by hour; it is the smooth rise rounded to whole twelfths","w":["the water climbs a twelfth of the range each hour for twelve hours; it is the average rate","the water climbs 3, 2, 1, 1, 2, 3 twelfths; it is the smooth rise turned about","the water climbs 2 twelfths each hour; it agrees with the smooth rise exactly"],"why":"The smooth rise gives 0.89, 2.22, 2.89, 2.89, 2.22, 0.89 twelfths. The rule is a law read from tides: it has a range and exceptions."},
 {"u":"13·nav.6.1","q":"Why does the rule fail at Seattle?","a":"that rise took 7 hours 43 minutes, not six, and ran quicker at its start than at its end; the rule and the smooth rise fit only a tide that rises the same both ways round","w":["Seattle's range is too small to divide into twelfths","Seattle's tide does not rise at all on that day","the rule needs heights in metres, and Seattle's table is in feet"],"why":"The water passed half its range before half the time had gone: 4.51 ft at 3 p.m. by the table against the smooth rise's 3.31."},
 {"u":"13·nav.7.1","q":"Why is “h′(t) = 6R(s − s²)” a move to watch out for?","a":"the factor 1/T is missing: s = (t − t₀)/T is an inside, and multiple-inside brings its slope 1/T","w":["the 6 should be a 3","the slope of 3s² − 2s³ is s − s², with no 6 at all","R should not be in the rate"],"why":"The slope in s is not the slope in t. The units show it too: feet, not feet an hour."},
 {"u":"13·nav.8.1","q":"Handed one hour of a six-hour rise under the rule of twelfths, how do you find the range?","a":"divide that hour's rise by its twelfths: a third hour of 1.8 ft is 3/12 of the range, so the range is 7.2 ft","w":["multiply that hour's rise by 6: a third hour of 1.8 ft means 10.8 ft","multiply that hour's rise by 12: a third hour of 1.8 ft means 21.6 ft","add that hour's rise to the low: 1.8 ft above L"],"why":"1.8 ÷ (3/12) = 7.2. Each hour's share is fixed by the rule, so any one hour gives the range."});
/* end phase F und_r13 */
/* phase F und_r16: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["16·nav"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["16·nav"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"16·nav.1.1","q":"What does dead-reckoning say, and where does the Monterey boat stand after three hours?","a":"s(T) = s(0) + ∫₀ᵀ v: where you are is where you started plus the area under your speed; the half-hour readings give about 22 nautical miles, off Santa Cruz","w":["s(T) = v(T)·T, the last speed times the time; about 21 nautical miles","s(T) = s(0) + v(0), the start plus the first speed; about 6.8 nautical miles","s(T) = ∫₀ᵀ v′, the area under the push; about 44 nautical miles"],"why":"It is net-change with Q = s. The left sum is 21.9 nmi and the right sum 22.0."},
 {"u":"16·nav.2.1","q":"What does speed-error say, and what does the log never see?","a":"if the log is within M of the true speed, the place is out by at most MT, a doubt that grows with the time run; the log reads speed through the water, so it never sees a current","w":["the place is out by exactly M whatever the time; the log never sees the wind","the place is out by at most M/T, shrinking with time; the log never sees the boat's own speed","the place is never out if the start is right; the log sees everything"],"why":"e(T) = ∫₀ᵀ (u − v), and area-bound gives |e(T)| ≤ MT. Nothing in the sums pulls the bound back."},
 {"u":"16·nav.3.1","q":"What does doubled-error say for an instrument that feels only its push?","a":"run backwards twice, a doubt of at most M in the acceleration puts the speed out by at most Mt and the place by at most MT²/2: it grows with the square of the time","w":["the place is out by at most MT, as for a log","the place is out by at most 2M, whatever the time","the two steps cancel, so the place is not out at all"],"why":"The speed's error is ∫(a_m − a), within Mt. The place's error is the area under that, and ∫₀ᵀ Mt dt = MT²/2."},
 {"u":"16·nav.4.1","q":"What happened at Honda Point in 1923, and what does it show?","a":"destroyers steaming on dead reckoning in fog turned east too early and ran onto the rocks, and seven were lost; a small doubt in speed grows to several nautical miles over a day's run","w":["a fleet in clear weather misread a chart; dead reckoning cannot go wrong","one destroyer ran out of fuel; a log is always exact at 20 knots","the ships turned too late and missed the channel; errors shrink over a long run"],"why":"They were north of their reckoning. At 20 knots, half a knot of doubt over a day is the width of the passage they aimed for."},
 {"u":"16·nav.5.1","q":"Why is “a doubt in the push grows with the time” a move to watch out for?","a":"it grows with the square of the time: a doubt in the acceleration is run backwards twice before it reaches the place","w":["it does not grow at all","it shrinks as the time runs on","it grows with the square root of the time"],"why":"Speed-error's MT is for a doubt in speed. Doubled-error gives MT²/2 for a doubt in the push."});
/* end phase F und_r16 */
/* phase F und_r18: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["18·nav"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["18·nav"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"18·nav.1.1","q":"What are easting and northing, and how far is Point Pinos, the origin, from the Santa Cruz light at (−8.4, 35.3)?","a":"how far east and how far north of a chosen origin a place lies, in one unit; √(8.4² + 35.3²) ≈ 36.3 km","w":["the direction and the distance of a place from the origin; 35.3 km","how far east and north a place lies; 8.4 + 35.3 = 43.7 km","the height and the slope of a place; 26.9 km"],"why":"Distance-laws on a flat grid: a county is a small enough patch of the round Earth for a flat grid to serve."},
 {"u":"18·nav.2.1","q":"What does map-scale say, and how can a map be checked?","a":"at scale 1 : n every paper distance is the ground distance over n and every paper area the ground area over n², so every triangle of places is similar to its picture; measure three places on the ground and on the paper, and if the three ratios differ the map is not at one scale","w":["paper distance and paper area are both the ground's over n; a map is checked by its date","at scale 1 : n, 1 cm stands for n metres; a map cannot be checked without a second map","every paper distance is the ground distance minus n; a map is checked by its scale bar alone"],"why":"A paper map is 18f's scaling X ↦ O + (1/n)(X − O), then a slide. A scale bar grows with a copy and stays right; the printed ratio does not."},
 {"u":"18·nav.3.1","q":"What two ways give a distance from a map, and why is a road never shorter?","a":"read the eastings and northings and use distance-laws, or lay a ruler across the paper and divide by the scale, and the two agree; a ruler gives the straight distance, and by the triangle technique a path made of segments is at least as long as the straight segment between its ends","w":["count the grid squares, or ask; a road can be shorter if it is straight in places","use distance-laws, or add Δe and Δn; a road is the same length as the ruler's line","use the ruler, or the contour lines; a road is shorter going downhill"],"why":"The right triangle with legs Δe and Δn is similar to its picture on the paper. Each bend can only add length."},
 {"u":"18·nav.4.1","q":"What does the surveyor's-triangle technique say, and how is a buoy fixed from a 600 m base?","a":"one measured length and two sighted directions fix a far point; with A = (0, 0) and B = (600, 0), the sight lines y = 1.5x and y = −3(x − 600) meet at T = (400, 600), by two-lines-meet","w":["two measured lengths and no directions fix a far point; T is the midpoint of the base, (300, 0)","one sighted direction fixes a far point; T = (100, 150), the first stake","three measured lengths are needed; the buoy cannot be fixed from the beach"],"why":"With angles, similar-triangles does the same on paper. A chain of such triangles is triangulation: only the first base is measured with a tape."},
 {"u":"18·nav.5.1","q":"What is a contour map, and how is it read?","a":"a flat drawing of heights: each contour line joins the places at one chosen height; along a line you neither climb nor fall, from one line to the next you climb or fall the same amount, and where the lines crowd together the ground is steep","w":["a drawing of roads by their widths; where lines crowd, the traffic is heavy","a side view of a hill; each line is a path to the top","a drawing where each line joins places equally far from the origin; where lines spread out, the ground is steep"],"why":"The height is a function from places (e, n) to numbers, and paper is flat. A graph of the height along one line across the map is the side view."},
 {"u":"18·nav.6.1","q":"Why is \"6 cm² at 1 : 24 000 is 6 × 240 m²\" a move to watch out for?","a":"an area scales by the square of the factor: 6 × 240² = 6 × 57 600 m², which is 34.56 ha","w":["the area is 6 × 24 000 m²","the area is 6 ÷ 240 m²","areas on a map do not scale at all"],"why":"Map-scale: paper area = ground area / n², by scaling in the plane."});
/* end phase F und_r18 */
/* phase F und_r20: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["20·nav"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["20·nav"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"20·nav.1.1","q":"What is a heading, and what does the heading-sum technique say?","a":"the direction the ship faces, in degrees, turning from north toward east; the heading after any turns is the first heading, plus the turns to starboard, less the turns to port, starting again past 360","w":["the direction to the ship's port of call; the heading after any turns is the product of the turns in degrees","the direction the ship faces, in degrees from east toward north; turns to port add","the direction the ship faces; the heading after any turns is the sum, which may pass 360 and stay there"],"why":"350° + 105° = 455°, and 455 − 360 = 095°."},
 {"u":"20·nav.2.1","q":"What is the pose of the ship, and what does the pose technique say?","a":"the move g(P) = R(P) + s, with R the turn of its facing and s its place; place and facing are one move of the plane, and unless the turn does nothing that move is a turn about one point","w":["the pair of its place and its speed; the pose is a slide","the move g(P) = R(P + s); the pose is always a turn about the origin","the move g(P) = R(P) + s; unless the turn does nothing, the pose is a glide"],"why":"Facing east, 2 miles east of the start, is the quarter turn to starboard about (1, −1)."},
 {"u":"20·nav.3.1","q":"Why is the pose after an order h, from the pose g, g ∘ h and not h ∘ g, and what does the voyage technique say?","a":"an order is given in the ship's own frame: it is done at the start and then carried by g; a voyage is its orders composed, first order on the left, its facing the product of the turns and its place adding each run turned by the facing at that moment","w":["an order is given on the chart: it is done after g; a voyage's place is the sum of its runs as given","an order is done at the start and then carried by g; a voyage's facing depends on the runs between the turns","the two orders agree, since poses turn about; a voyage is its orders in any order"],"why":"g ∘ h has turn part R_g R_h and place s_g + R_g s_h."},
 {"u":"20·nav.4.1","q":"What does the order technique say, and why can a turn and a run not be swapped?","a":"runs can be done in any order, but a turn and a run cannot: the heading comes out the same and the place is off by the run turned less the run, Rs − s, which is 0 only when one of them does nothing","w":["any two orders can be swapped; the place comes out the same","runs cannot be swapped but turns can; the place is off by the sum of the runs","a turn and a run cannot be swapped: the place agrees and the heading is off"],"why":"\"Ahead 2, starboard 90\" ends 2 north, and \"starboard 90, ahead 2\" ends 2 east, both facing east."},
 {"u":"20·nav.5.1","q":"Why is \"350° + 30° = 380°\" a move to watch out for?","a":"headings start again past 360: the heading is 020°","w":["turns to starboard take away: the heading is 320°","the heading stays at 360°, due north","380° is right; the move to watch is writing it 020°"],"why":"A full turn of 360° is doing nothing, like 4j's clock."});
/* end phase F und_r20 */
/* phase F und_r21: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["21·nav"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["21·nav"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"21·nav.1.1","q":"Why is the height of the pole star above the horizon your latitude?","a":"its rays run along the earth's axis; up makes 90° − φ with the axis, and the horizon is square to up, so the horizon makes 90° − (90° − φ) = φ with the axis","w":["the pole star stands straight overhead at every latitude","its rays run along the equator, so its height is 90° − φ","the horizon is parallel to the axis, so the star's height is 0 until you reach the pole"],"why":"Latitude is the angle at the centre between your place and the equator, and the axis is square to the equator."},
 {"u":"21·nav.2.1","q":"What does the two-mirror technique say, and why does turning one mirror by β read 2β?","a":"turning one mirror by β turns the seen star by 2β, so an arc of 60° reads angles up to 120°; each bounce flips the ray across its mirror, and two flips across lines meeting at β make a turn by 2β","w":["turning one mirror by β turns the seen star by β; each bounce turns the ray by half","turning one mirror by β turns the seen star by β²; two flips multiply","turning one mirror by β turns the seen star by 2β, because the ship rolls as far again"],"why":"Crossing-flips (20e)."},
 {"u":"21·nav.3.1","q":"What does the noon technique say, and how is noon found without a clock?","a":"your latitude is 90° less the noon sun's height, plus the day's δ: φ = 90° − h + δ; noon is the moment the sun stops climbing","w":["your latitude is the noon sun's height; noon is when the sun is due east","your latitude is 90° less the noon sun's height, less δ; noon is when shadows are longest","your latitude is δ; noon is found only with a clock"],"why":"The noon sun is φ − δ from overhead, and height is the complement of that."},
 {"u":"21·nav.4.1","q":"What does the reckoning technique say, and what is a nautical mile?","a":"heading and speed carried forward as vectors give your place between sightings, a run moving vt sin θ east and vt cos θ north, and its error grows with every run; a nautical mile is one minute of latitude","w":["a run moves vt cos θ east and vt sin θ north, with no error; a nautical mile is a minute of longitude","headings and speeds are added as numbers; a nautical mile is a thousand paces","a run moves vt sin θ east and vt cos θ north; a nautical mile is one degree of latitude"],"why":"Headings turn from north toward east, so east is the sine."},
 {"u":"21·nav.5.1","q":"Why is \"a nautical mile is a minute of longitude\" a move to watch out for?","a":"it is a minute of latitude; lines of longitude close in toward the poles, so a minute of longitude is not one length","w":["it is a second of latitude","a minute of longitude is longer than a minute of latitude everywhere","it is a minute of either, since the earth is a ball"],"why":"A line of latitude at φ is a circle of radius R cos φ (21·cb18)."});
/* end phase F und_r21 */
/* phase F und_r22: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["22·nav"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["22·nav"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"22·nav.1.1","q":"By how much does a straight-grid map stretch an east-west length at latitude φ, and why?","a":"by sec φ: the circle of latitude has length 2πR cos φ on the globe and is drawn as wide as the equator, 2πR","w":["by cos φ: the circle of latitude shrinks toward the pole","by tan φ: the map's height over its width","by 2: every circle of latitude is half the equator"],"why":"At 60° N the stretch is 2; at the pole sec is not defined."},
 {"u":"22·nav.2.1","q":"At what height must a straight-grid map draw latitude φ to keep every heading, and why must the north stretch be sec φ?","a":"at R ln(sec φ + tan φ), the total of thin strips each stretched by sec φ; a drawn step has tan β′ = (sec φ/k) tan β, so headings are kept exactly when the north stretch k equals the east stretch sec φ","w":["at R sin φ; the north stretch must be cos φ to undo the east stretch","at Rφ; the north stretch is 1, since lines of latitude are evenly spaced","at R sec φ; the north stretch must be sec² φ to keep areas"],"why":"On this map a steady compass heading is a straight line, and a ruler lays the course."},
 {"u":"22·nav.3.1","q":"What happens to the drawn height on a heading-keeping map as φ nears the pole, and which sum does the proof lean on?","a":"it passes every bound; near the pole sec φ ≥ 1/c with c the angle from the pole, so strips of width h add to at least R(½ + ⅓ + ⋯ + 1/n), the harmonic sum","w":["it settles at R ln 2; the proof leans on 1 − ½ + ⅓ − ⋯","it passes every bound; the proof leans on a geometric sum of ratio 2","it settles at 2πR; the proof leans on the p-sum with p = 2"],"why":"So the pole falls off the top of the map."},
 {"u":"22·nav.4.1","q":"What can a straight-grid map keep, and where does it keep both?","a":"every heading (k = sec φ) or every size (k sec φ = 1), but both only where sec² φ = 1, along the equator; and no flat map at all keeps every distance","w":["every heading and every size, everywhere, if its strips are thin enough","every size or every distance, and both only at the poles","every heading or every size, and both along every line of longitude"],"why":"The heading map stretches areas by sec² φ: Greenland is drawn about 10.5 times too large."},
 {"u":"22·nav.5.1","q":"What is out of place in “a straight line on the heading map is the shortest route”?","a":"a straight line there is a steady heading; the shortest route is the great circle, which bends toward the pole on this map","w":["a straight line there is a great circle, which is the longest route","the shortest route is along a line of latitude","nothing: straight lines are shortest on every map"],"why":"The map keeps headings, not distances."});
/* end phase F und_r22 */
/* phase F und_r23: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["23·nav"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["23·nav"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"23·nav.1.1","q":"How long is the line of sight to the horizon from height h above a ball of radius R, and which two facts prove it?","a":"√(2Rh + h²); the line that touches a circle is square to the radius there, and the right-triangle technique gives (R + h)² = R² + d²","w":["√(2Rh); the arc technique and the small-angle technique","2Rh + h²; the right-triangle technique alone","R + h; the line of sight is a radius"],"why":"Multiply out and R² cancels."},
 {"u":"23·nav.2.1","q":"About how far is the horizon, how is the dropped term held to account, and how does the distance over the water compare?","a":"about √(2Rh): dropping h² shortens it by a share less than h/(4R), from 1 ≤ √(1 + x) ≤ 1 + x/2; the distance over the water is a little shorter than the sight line, by a share less than h/R","w":["exactly √(2Rh); the distance over the water is longer than the sight line","about √(2Rh), with no bound on what was dropped; the two distances are equal","about 2Rh; dropping h² shortens it by half"],"why":"Every number on that page is a fence, and its width is a share of h/R."},
 {"u":"23·nav.3.1","q":"At what distance does a light at height h₁ first show to an eye at height h₂, and why do the heights sit under separate roots?","a":"at about √(2Rh₁) + √(2Rh₂); at the farthest place the line grazes the sea at one point T, and the two sight lines to T lie on one line, so their lengths add","w":["at about √(2R(h₁ + h₂)); the heights add before the root","at about √(2Rh₁) − √(2Rh₂); the eye's horizon is taken off","at about √(2Rh₁); the eye's height does not matter"],"why":"Each sight line has its own right triangle."},
 {"u":"23·nav.4.1","q":"What does air that bends the sight line along a circle of radius ρ do to the sea, and where does the doubt in the horizon's distance live?","a":"it makes the sea look like a ball of radius R′, with 1/R′ = 1/R − 1/ρ; the doubt lives in ρ, an average over many days of weather: the horizon's distance is a fence, and the weather sets its width","w":["it makes the sea look like a ball of radius R + ρ; the doubt lives in the dropped h² term","it makes the sea look smaller, 1/R′ = 1/R + 1/ρ; there is no doubt, since ρ = 7R","it changes nothing; the doubt lives in R"],"why":"The sags subtract: x²/(2R) less x²/(2ρ)."},
 {"u":"23·nav.5.1","q":"What is out of place in “twice the height, twice the distance”, and in “D = √(2R(h₁ + h₂))”?","a":"the distance goes as √h: four times the height doubles it; and two sight lines add, each under its own root","w":["twice the height gives four times the distance; and the heights multiply under the root","nothing in the first; in the second R should be doubled","the distance does not depend on height; and D = √(2Rh₁h₂)"],"why":"A root is taken of each height, not of their sum."});
/* end phase F und_r23 */
/* phase F und_r27: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["27·nav"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27·nav"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27·nav.1.1","q":"What does the travel-time technique say, and why can timing alone not fix a phone?","a":"a distance is the speed of light times the delay, so each billionth of a second of error is about 30 cm; the phone’s quartz clock may be a thousandth of a second off, which makes every distance 300 km wrong","w":["a distance is the delay over the speed of light; timing fails because the satellites’ clocks drift","each billionth of a second is about 30 km; timing fails because light bends","a distance is the speed of light times the delay; timing alone is enough once three satellites are heard"],"why":"The satellites carry atomic clocks; the phone does not."},
 {"u":"27·nav.2.1","q":"What does the sphere-difference technique say, and why must the answer be checked on one sphere?","a":"taking one sphere’s sameness from another’s cancels every square, and the points on both spheres lie on the straight sameness left; but the straight samenesses alone may hold points on no sphere, so one sphere is kept as a check","w":["adding two spheres’ samenesses cancels the squares; no check is needed","the difference of two spheres is a third sphere; it is checked against the first","subtracting cancels the centres; the answer is checked because the squares come back"],"why":"Subtracting keeps every true point and may let in false ones."},
 {"u":"27·nav.3.1","q":"What does the clock-unknown technique say, and why do the answers of the straight samenesses lie along a line?","a":"with the clock error as a fourth unknown, four satellites give three straight samenesses, and one sphere then picks the point by a quadratic; three samenesses in four unknowns leave one free choice, so their answers form a line","w":["three satellites give three straight samenesses with one answer; the clock is found afterwards","four satellites give four straight samenesses with exactly one answer; no sphere is needed","the clock error is measured, not solved for; the answers lie along a line because satellites move"],"why":"The phone learns its place and its clock error in one stroke."},
 {"u":"27·nav.4.1","q":"What does the spread-sky technique say, and why is a fix a fence?","a":"an error in the distances moves the fix by the undo of the grid, so when the satellites bunch and the determinant nears 0 the same small error moves the fix much further; so a fix is reported with its width: the distances’ fence, stretched by how the satellites stand","w":["bunched satellites give the best fix; a fix is one point","the fix moves by the grid itself, so a large determinant is worst; the fence is the same everywhere","the error in the distances never reaches the fix; the fence is the size of the phone"],"why":"Spread across the sky, the same error moves the fix least."},
 {"u":"27·nav.5.1","q":"What is wrong with “three satellites fix a phone”, and with “ρᵢ is the distance to satellite i”?","a":"the phone’s clock error is a fourth unknown, so four are needed; and ρᵢ is the distance plus the clock error b, the same for every satellite","w":["two satellites are enough on the ground; and ρᵢ is the delay","three are enough once the squares are cancelled; and ρᵢ is the distance exactly","five are needed, one for each unknown; and ρᵢ is the distance less the height"],"why":"Four unknowns: three of place, one of time."});
/* end phase F und_r27 */
/* phase F und_r29: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["29·nav"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["29·nav"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"29·nav.1.1","q":"Which triangle gives the route from one city to another, what are its sides and its angle at the pole, and why does the shortest route from Seattle to Tokyo set off north of west?","a":"the triangle of the pole and the two cities, with sides 90° less each latitude and the difference of longitude as the angle at the pole; the shortest route is the great circle through both, which bows toward the pole, where the circles of latitude are shorter","w":["the flat triangle on the map; the route sets off south of west because Tokyo is south","the triangle of the two cities and the earth’s centre; the route holds one heading","the triangle of the pole and the cities with sides equal to the latitudes; the route sets off north because of the wind"],"why":"About 6% shorter than holding one heading."},
 {"u":"29·nav.2.1","q":"What does the kept-product technique say, and when does a great-circle route run through the pole?","a":"along a great-circle route cos φ times sin h is the same at every point; when the product is 0 the route leaves due north or south and runs through the pole, which happens when the far city’s longitude is the same or 180° away","w":["cos φ plus sin h is kept; a route runs through the pole when it starts on the equator","sin φ times cos h is kept; no great-circle route passes the pole","the heading h is kept; a route runs through the pole when h = 45°"],"why":"Where sin h = 1 the route is at its highest latitude."},
 {"u":"29·nav.3.1","q":"What does the star-circle technique say, and why is the pole star’s height the latitude?","a":"every place that sees a star at height H lies on the circle of points 90° − H from the star’s ground point, so a navigator works out H for a guessed place and moves toward the star by the difference, a nautical mile for each minute of arc; the pole star’s ground point is the pole, 90° − φ away, so H = φ","w":["every place that sees a star at height H lies on a line toward the star; the pole star’s height is 90° everywhere","a star’s height gives the longitude; the pole star’s height is 90° less the latitude","every place sees every star at the same height; the pole star’s height is the time"],"why":"A second star gives a second line, and the ship is where they cross."},
 {"u":"29·nav.4.1","q":"How does a triangle on the earth say which geometry the earth has?","a":"its angles add to more than two right angles, and the excess, read with the area, gives the radius; which geometry a surface has is a measured fact, within what the instruments can see","w":["its angles add to exactly two right angles, which shows the ground is flat","its sides are longer than on a map, which gives the radius","no triangle on the earth can be measured from the surface"],"why":"A 30 km triangle has an excess of about two seconds of arc."},
 {"u":"29·nav.5.1","q":"What does the half-angle distance technique say, and why is it used in place of cos γ?","a":"the square of the sine of half the distance is the square of the sine of half the latitude difference plus cos φ₁ cos φ₂ times the square of the sine of half the longitude difference; for short distances cos γ is so near 1 that rounding wipes the distance out, while this form never subtracts two nearly equal numbers","w":["the sine of the distance is the sum of the sines of the differences; it is used because it is quicker","half the distance is the average of the two differences; cos γ is wrong for long distances","it is the same as cos γ to every place; it is used by habit"],"why":"Space Needle to Pike Place: cos γ rounds to 1 at seven figures; the half-angle form gives 1.31 km."},
 {"u":"29·nav.6.1","q":"What is wrong with “a great-circle route holds one heading”?","a":"along a great circle the heading changes all the way, with cos φ sin h kept; the path of one heading is a straight line on 22·nav’s map and a longer curve on the globe","w":["a great circle holds its heading except at the pole","a great circle holds one heading only when flown westward","nothing; straightest means one heading"],"why":"Seattle to Tokyo starts at 300.3°; the steady heading would be 260.8°."});
/* end phase F und_r29 */
/* phase F und_r30: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30·nav"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30·nav"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30·nav.1.1","q":"What does the weigh-by-trust technique say?","a":"two independent fixes are best combined with weights in proportion to one over their spreads, and the result is then better than either fix","w":["two fixes are best combined by a plain average, which always beats either","the better fix should be used alone, since the worse can only harm it","two fixes are best combined with weights in proportion to their spreads"],"why":"The wider fix still tells something, so it gets a small weight, not none."},
 {"u":"30·nav.2.1","q":"What does the trusts-add technique say, and when does a plain average of two fixes beat the better fix alone?","a":"with weights in proportion to one over each spread, one over the spread of the result is the sum of one over each spread; a plain average beats the better fix only when the worse is less than √3 times as wide","w":["the spreads of the two fixes add; a plain average always beats the better fix","one over the width of the result is the sum of one over each width; a plain average beats the better fix when the worse is less than twice as wide","one over the spread of the result is the sum of one over each spread; a plain average never beats the better fix"],"why":"A 2 m fix and a 10 m fix averaged plainly give 5.1 m, worse than 2 m."},
 {"u":"30·nav.3.1","q":"What does the predict-and-correct technique say?","a":"between fixes the spread of a carried position grows by q each minute, and each fix brings it back by the weigh-by-trust technique, so the spread settles into a saw tooth whose low point solves P² + qtP − qtR = 0","w":["the spread grows without bound, since each fix adds its own error","each fix brings the spread to 0, and it climbs from 0 again","the spread settles to the spread R of the fixes and stays level there"],"why":"With q = 225 m² a minute and 10 m fixes every 5 minutes: 9.6 m just after a fix, 34.9 m just before the next."},
 {"u":"30·nav.4.1","q":"What does the circle-chance technique say, and how do errors at right angles add?","a":"with errors east and north independent bells of width σ, the chance of being more than r off is e^(−r²/(2σ²)); the squares of the two errors add to the square of the distance off","w":["the chance of being more than r off is e^(−r/σ); the two errors add","the chance of being within r is e^(−r²/(2σ²)); the squares of the errors add","the chance of being more than r off is 1 − r/σ; the larger error is the distance off"],"why":"The 95% circle has radius σ√(2 ln 20) = 2.45σ."},
 {"u":"30·nav.5.1","q":"Why is “weights in proportion to one over the width” a move to watch out for?","a":"the best weights go as one over the spread, the width squared; weights by one over the width give the wider fix too much say","w":["the best weights go as the width","the best weights are equal","nothing is wrong; width and spread give the same weights"],"why":"For widths of 2 m and 10 m the weights are 25 to 1, not 5 to 1."});
/* end phase F und_r30 */
/* phase F und_r32: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["32·nav"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["32·nav"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"32·nav.1.1","q":"Which motions fix the speeds of the tide’s turnings, why is the tide not periodic, and what is left to measure?","a":"the Earth’s turning under the Moon’s and the Sun’s bulges, the tilt of their paths and the Moon’s changing distance; the speeds, such as 28.984 and 30, are not whole multiples of one speed, so no single repeat fits; at each port a size Aₖ and a timing gₖ for each turning","w":["the wind and the pressure fix the speeds; the tide repeats every 12 hours; nothing is left to measure","the Moon alone fixes one speed; the tide repeats every 12.42 hours; the speed is left to measure at each port","the sky fixes the sizes and timings; the speeds must be measured at each port"],"why":"A finite sum of turnings with known speeds, not a series of 32a."},
 {"u":"32·nav.2.1","q":"What does the long-record technique say, and how long a record separates the main lunar and main solar turnings?","a":"over a record of length T the product of two turnings whose speeds differ by Δω has size at most 2/(|Δω|T), so they are nearly at right angles once T is long against 2π/|Δω|; for these two that time is 14.77 days, the time they take to drift one whole turn apart","w":["turnings of different speeds are exactly at right angles over any record; one day is enough","the product has size at most 2/(|Δω|T); the two are separated after 12.42 hours, one lunar repeat","the product grows with T; no record separates them"],"why":"1/(1/12 − 1/12.4206) hours."},
 {"u":"32·nav.3.1","q":"Why do the main lunar and main solar turnings make spring and neap tides, and what sets their sizes?","a":"they are close in speed and unequal in size, so they beat: the sum’s size swings from A₁ + A₂ when they are in step, at full and new moon, to A₁ − A₂ at the quarters, once every 14.77 days; at Seattle about 1.34 m and 0.80 m","w":["the Moon comes nearer once a month; springs are A₁ and neaps A₂","they have the same speed and add to one fixed size A₁ + A₂","they beat between 2A₁ and 0, since beats always fall to nothing"],"why":"The second turning goes round the first."},
 {"u":"32·nav.4.1","q":"How is a tide predicted once the sizes and timings are known, and how are the exceptions handled?","a":"by adding a few dozen cosines Aₖ cos(ωₖt − gₖ) for any time wanted; storms add a surge that no turning holds, and its sizes and how often they come are recorded beside the table and stated as a chance","w":["by running the gauge forward; there are no exceptions","by adding the cosines; surges are added to the table as one more turning","by repeating last year’s record; exceptions are left out of the record"],"why":"A law with a known exception rate."},
 {"u":"32·nav.5.1","q":"Why is “a week of record is enough to find the size of the main solar turning” a move to watch out for?","a":"over a week the solar and lunar turnings are far from right angles; they take about 15 days to drift a whole turn apart, and until then their shadows cannot be told apart","w":["the solar turning repeats only once a year","a week is enough, but only at full moon","no record of any length separates two turnings"],"why":"T must be long against 2π/|Δω|."});
/* end phase F und_r32 */
